  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 227 PM CDT Tue Sep 8 2026\n\n19Z water vapor imagery showed the upper ridge was still over the \nsouthern plains as a series of upper level waves moved across the \nPacific Northwest and northern plains. Surface obs placed a weak \nboundary from northwest KS through the middle MO river valley and \ninto MN. \n\nFor tonight through Wednesday night, the weak boundary is going to \nbe the main factor in the forecast regarding temps and precip \nchances. There is pretty good agreement in the operational solutions \nfor shortwave energy to pass north of the forecast area late \ntonight. This should help slowly push the surface front into the \narea and eventually south of the forecast area Wednesday. Since most \nof the dynamics are north, precip chances will be tied mainly to \ninstability and convergence along the front. Am a little hesitant to \ngo with the categorical POPs from the NBM since CAMs tend to show \nmore discreet storms. The exception to this is Wednesday night with \nthe boundary should still be near east central KS as the low level \njet kicks in. Have left some higher POPs in the forecast for this \nperiod. There is a low potential for severe storms this evening and \nagain Wednesday afternoon as MUCAPES increase to near 4000 J/kg. But \nthe boundary is progged to remain south of the better westerlies \nboth tonight and Wednesday afternoon resulting in meager deep layer \nshear. So I anticipate some pulse storm activity could occur and be \ncapable of damaging winds and perhaps some hail during the \ninitiation of convection. As long as storms remain rather discreet, \nflooding concerns should be limited. But there is forecast to be \nplenty of moisture available with a deep warm layer aiding in warm \nrain processes. So it is not out of the questions some isolated rain \namounts between 1 and 2 inches. \n\nAs for temperatures Wednesday, models show highs could potentially \nget into the upper 90s across Coffey and Anderson counties before \nthe boundary moves through. This would push heat indices back into \nthe triple digits for parts of east central Kansas. But I think \nincreasing cloud cover and the prospects for convection turning over \nthe boundary layer could limit the duration of any temperatures like \nthis and have held off on issuing a heat advisory for Coffey and \nAnderson counties. Later shifts can take another look at this. \n\nSome of the nicest weather is expected for Thursday before another \nboundary moves through the central plains Friday night and Saturday. \nThis should bring another chance for showers and thunderstorms. But \nThursday should see highs in the 80s with a little less humidity. \nSoutherly return flow Friday is expected to push temps back into the \nlower 90s. Will need to watch out for some possible warm air \nadvection storms late Thursday night and Friday morning. The 06Z NAM \nwas wanting to develop some elevated storms, but the most recent \nguidance shows the mid level moisture is a little lacking within the \nwarm air advection pattern. So the forecast remains dry for now. \n\nBy Sunday through Tuesday, the 00Z ensemble data starts to show \ngreater spread is potential outcomes with some disagreement in the \namplitude of the shortwave activity. Temperatures spreads by this \ntime increase to betweeen 10 and 15 degrees while the location and \ntiming of a surface front varying as well. So while the 12Z \noperational GFS/ECMWF seem to be in reasonable agreement, there is a \nlittle lower predictability than the operational solutions would \nsuggest. Have stuck with the NBM/WPC forecast which has a broad \nbrush of POPs and temps that are a little more seasonal.",
